## Waiting on user — exact launch and touchpad test instructions

1. Open a normal, interactive PowerShell in the repository root and run:
   ```powershell
   powershell -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File .\spikes\B\launch-probe.ps1
   ```
   This launches the existing offline-built executable and appends diagnostics to docs/spikes/B/input-probe.tsv. Do not run build.ps1 or enable network to do the physical test. If no visible window titled/content-labelled "AntiQuickPad B — Gate 1 input probe" appears within 15 seconds, stop and tell the director "B has no visible window"; do not perform the touchpad sequence. The title may be blank in the process list, so identify the content label.
2. Click inside the large editor area and type `OK`. Confirm text appears and caret remains there. Move the mouse pointer back over the editor (not the header/log area). F-keys label trials without consuming the editor's normal input; use Fn+F-key if needed. After F1, confirm a SLOW_PAN marker appears in the recent-event area; if no marker appears, stop and report that.
3. F1 — SLOW_PAN: with two fingers, slowly pan horizontally right, then left, keeping contact during each move; lift between repetitions. Do three passes. Keep pointer over the editor. Note any missing events or vertical interference.
4. F2 — PAN_PAUSE_LIFT: pan horizontally about 2 cm, hold both fingers motionless on the pad for two full seconds, then lift both fingers without a flick. Wait two seconds. Repeat three times. Also touch and lift without movement once; absence of a recognized gesture in this last case is recorded as a limitation, not proof of lift support.
5. F3 — FAST_FLICK: make a fast horizontal two-finger flick and lift so inertia can continue; do not re-touch until motion ends, then wait two seconds. Repeat left and right three times. Note whether OS inertia is observable; if this device/settings produces no inertia, say so (do not change settings just for the test).
6. F4 — INTERRUPT_INERTIA: flick horizontally, then re-touch while inertia is still happening and pan in the opposite direction. Repeat twice. This distinguishes interruption from ordinary completion.
7. F5 — VERTICAL_SCROLL: two-finger scroll down/up through the 70-line fixture. Confirm ordinary vertical editor scrolling works. F6 — TEXT_SELECTION: mouse-drag a text selection, type `selection test`, then Ctrl+Z; confirm the editor still behaves normally. These are arbitration checks, not proof of the later carousel.
8. F7 — HORIZONTAL_WHEEL: if a horizontal/tilt wheel is available, use it over the editor in both directions. If unavailable, mark N/A; do not treat touchpad input as this test. F8 — COMPLETE, then close the probe window to finish the trace.
9. Reply "B input test complete" with: whether a window/caret appeared; whether F-key markers appeared; whether typing, vertical scrolling and selection/undo worked; whether inertia was observable; any visible oddity; and whether a horizontal wheel was available. Mention if this was a Windows Precision Touchpad or you are unsure. Keep the local trace file in place; no upload is needed.

