# Spike B report — T-003

Updated: 2026-10-17. Owner: spike-b-director.
**Build passes; revised Gate 1 wheel/mouse test awaits user trace.** No probe launch or Computer Use attempted this resume.

## Current gate and verdict

The manager revised [spike spec item 4a](../../spike-spec.md) and the final [decision entry](../../decisions-log.md): the user has no touchpad. Primary inputs are a notched horizontal wheel and mouse drag with real button release. Wheel release is a tunable idle gap (~80–150ms), subject to measured cadence; touchpad lift/inertia is optional. This supersedes the earlier touchpad gate.

Manager-provided Spike A observation: WM_MOUSEHWHEEL delta=120 per notch, about 80–170ms apart, no hi-res deltas. These are **not B measurements**. Mouse model/driver is unknown and requested in the physical-test instructions. Smooth/hi-res wheels and driver inertia remain untested on unavailable hardware. No settings changed.

Would I build the real app on this? **Decision pending B's input trace and remaining capability probes.** The earlier stock touchpad phase limitation is no longer the gate. No carousel or performance claim follows from a successful build.

## Build and source evidence

Manager reports clean pinned hello build PASS in 2m28s; lockfile/cache/target/exe verified. Rust/cargo 1.99.1 MSVC, CMake 4.4.4, VS2026 Community C++ tools compiled this debug stack successfully without a VS2022 fallback. Kit/component 0.7.3 and GPUI 0.3.9 remain pinned; default backend retained.

The updated probe passes "'`cargo build --offline --locked` using `CARGO_HOME=spikes/B/.cargo-home`, **8.2175757s incremental**, including the Windows diagnostic rebuild. [Build log](build-probe.log), [timing](build-probe-result.txt). No network used. Rust formatting checked. Prior launch/build evidence remains in logs and `.coord/status-B.md`.

Restored GPUI Windows package metadata identifies Zed revision 93d745668805029476e6d7fbb19652116fed51de. [Exact-source audit](input-source-audit.md), [diagnostic patch](native-diagnostic.patch). Original cache is unchanged; vendored Apache license retained. The diagnostic seam now touches two Windows files: historical optional Direct Manipulation status logging in direct_manipulation.rs, and raw wheel logging in events.rs. Other vendored files match the original.

## Trace contract

All lines have a microsecond Unix timestamp; the probe appends to docs/spikes/B/input-probe.tsv. Logging is non-consuming capture before normal editor event handling; note contents/arbitrary typed keys are not recorded.

- PUBLIC_SCROLL: public phase, units (lines/pixels), dx/dy, position, **editor_focus**, and normalized notch_steps_x/y. Read-only SystemParametersInfoW queries use the same scroll-char/line settings as the pinned Windows backend; unsupported/zero/page modes or pixel events report NA. Fractional steps are retained. Normalized steps are not assertions about physical detents.
- NATIVE_WHEEL: exact WM_MOUSEHWHEEL / WM_MOUSEWHEEL raw signed delta and notch_delta=raw_delta/120. Pair with the following public event for focus/context. The pinned backend divides horizontal raw delta by -120 and multiplies by Windows scroll chars; vertical uses +120 and scroll lines. Thus public horizontal sign is opposite raw WM_MOUSEHWHEEL.
- MOUSE_PRESS / MOUSE_DRAG_MOVE / MOUSE_RELEASE: button, logical position and actual editor_focus; moves are logged only with a button held. No gesture event is consumed. The first focus click can legitimately log false before editor focus changes.
- MARKER: F7 HORIZONTAL_WHEEL, F5 VERTICAL_SCROLL, F6 TEXT_SELECTION, **F9 DRAG**, F8 COMPLETE, each with editor_focus. Historical optional F1–F4 touchpad markers remain available but are not requested.

The fixture is a soft-wrapped Kit Editor with 70 lines. The short test in [.coord/status-B.md](../../../.coord/status-B.md) covers slow/fast wheel notches both directions (three passes each), vertical wheel, selection/undo, held-left-button drag both directions, then completion.

